  "question": "Which chargers are missing from the alert queue?",
  "mode": "live",
  "answer": "All of them. As of 4 September 2026, detection flagged 26 chargers as impaired while their status reporting showed them healthy, and none of the 26 appear anywhere in the operations alert queue (123 open alerts across 106 devices). The reason is structural: the existing rules watch the fault log, so they can only surface conditions that produced a fault. These units reported themselves available throughout.\n\nThree of the 26 episodes were still running at the as-of date. In dispatch priority order (still-running episodes first, then by daily loss rate) they are: CHG-0034 at Birmingham Motorway 006, reporting 100% uptime while delivering nothing across 63 attempts since 2 September; CHG-0501 at Leicester Airport 127, delivering on 36.62% of 71 attempts against a same-site peer rate of 95.8% since 30 August; and CHG-0023 at Birmingham City Centre 004, delivering on 39.53% of 43 attempts against a peer rate of 92.52% since 1 September.\n\nEstimated revenue at risk across all 26 detected silent failures is \u00a312,648.95 for the observed period (6 August to 4 September 2026), with \u00a3707.92 per day attached to the three episodes still running. Those are prioritisation estimates, not accounting-grade lost revenue.\n\nTwo limits worth noting: detection is deliberately conservative, so short or low-traffic episodes fall below the thresholds and are missed, and this list covers only failures hidden behind a healthy status. It says nothing about whether the 123 existing alerts are being worked. This is a synthetic demonstration estate, not real operator data.",
